.Dd June 28, 2019
.Dt SHM_OPEN 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm memfd_create ,
.Nm shm_open ,
.Nm shm_unlink
.Nd shared memory object operations
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/types.h
.In sys/mman.h
.Ft int
.Fn memfd_create "const char *name" "unsigned int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn shm_open "const char *path" "int flags" "mode_t mode"
.Ft int
.Fn shm_unlink "const char *path"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn shm_open
function opens (or optionally creates) a
POSIX
shared memory object named
.Fa path .
The
.Fn shm_unlink
function removes a shared memory object named
.Fa path .
Using the same
.Fa path
allows unrelated processes to access the same object.
.Pp
The
.Fn memfd_create
function creates an anonymous shared memory object and returns a file
descriptor referring to it.
The object has no pathname and is not visible in the shared memory
namespace used by
.Fn shm_open .
Newly created objects have a size of zero.
Writes to a memfd automatically grow it to cover the requested range.
Bytes in a gap before the written range read as zero.
The size can also be adjusted with
.Xr ftruncate 2 .
Objects created by
.Fn shm_open
do not automatically grow on write.
.Pp
The
.Fa name
argument must point to a valid null-terminated string no longer than
.Dv NAME_MAX
characters.
The name is used for diagnostic purposes only and does not identify the
object.
Names do not need to be unique.
.Pp
The following
.Fa flags
may be specified:
.Bl -tag -width MFD_ALLOW_SEALING
.It Dv MFD_CLOEXEC
Set
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
on the returned file descriptor.
.It Dv MFD_ALLOW_SEALING
Not currently supported.
.It Dv MFD_HUGETLB
Not currently supported.
.El
.Pp
Any object created by
.Fn memfd_create
can be resized with
.Xr ftruncate 2 ,
read and written with
.Xr read 2
and
.Xr write 2 ,
and mapped with
.Xr mmap 2 .
Both
.Dv MAP_SHARED
and
.Dv MAP_PRIVATE
mappings are supported.
Private modifications do not change the shared object.
A read-only descriptor permits writable private mappings, but not writable
shared mappings.
The maximum protection of a shared mapping is limited by the descriptor's
access mode, including later changes with
.Xr mprotect 2 .
.Pp
A mapping may extend beyond the current size of the object.
Access to a page wholly beyond the current end of the object faults;
on DragonFly x86-64 this delivers
.Dv SIGSEGV ,
rather than the
.Dv SIGBUS
delivered by FreeBSD.
The unused portion of the final partial page is initially zero-filled.
Extending the object makes the newly covered pages accessible through
existing mappings.
Truncation removes shared pages beyond the new size.
Mapping the object updates its access timestamp.
.Pp
The descriptor can be shared with another process through
.Xr fork 2
or
.Xr sendmsg 2 .
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
.Dx
implements these operations with
.Xr shmfd 9
objects.
Named objects are kept in a kernel-managed shared-memory namespace and are
not vnode-backed files, so no mountpoint is required.
The namespace is scoped by the caller's jail; objects in different jails do
not collide even when they use the same
.Fa path .
The
.Fa path
for a named object must begin with
.Ql \&/ .
The
.Fa mode
argument is recorded as the initial object permission mode after applying
the process creation mask.
The
.Fn shm_open
library function adds
.Dv O_CLOEXEC
and then calls the kernel's
.Fn shm_open2
operation.
The internal
.Fn shm_open2
operation also accepts
.Dv SHM_ANON
to create an unnamed object.
The
.Fn memfd_create
library function instead invokes the
.Dv SYS_memfd_create
system call directly; it does not route through
.Fn shm_open2 .
The
.Fa name
passed to
.Fn memfd_create
is diagnostic metadata only and is not added to the named namespace.
The kernel copies this string directly from the caller, so an invalid pointer
causes
.Dv EFAULT .
.Pp
Unlinking a named object removes its namespace entry.
Open descriptors and mappings continue to hold references to the object until
they are closed or unmapped.
The
.Fn shm_unlink
operation does not require a vnode or a filesystem directory entry.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
If successful,
.Fn memfd_create
and
.Fn shm_open
return a non-negative integer;
.Fn shm_unlink
returns zero.
All functions return -1 on failure, and set
.Va errno
to indicate the error.
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
.Fn memfd_create
is a
.Dx
extension compatible with the common Linux interface of
the same name.
Unlike a pathname-based shared memory object, a memfd has no name that
can be passed to
.Fn shm_open
or removed with
.Fn shm_unlink .
.Pp
On many other operating systems the
.Fa path
argument is interpreted as a file system pathname under a special
directory where a memory-backed file system is mounted.
Most operating systems do some name mangling to
.Fa path .
Leading slashes are commonly removed to turn an absolute pathname into
a relative one.
Problematic characters may be escaped and there may be a length limit on
.Fa path .
On some systems the mangled pathname is completely different from the
given
.Fa path .
On a few systems, shared memory objects live outside the ordinary file
system in their own dedicated namespace.
.Pp
According to
POSIX
two processes opening the same
.Fa path
are guaranteed to access the same shared memory object if and only if
.Fa path
begins with a slash.
The most portable form of pathname is probably
.Ql /foobar ,
i.e.\& one leading slash, no other slashes and no dots.
.Pp
The result of using
.Xr open 2
on the pathname of a shared memory object,
or using
.Xr read 2
or
.Xr write 2
on a file descriptor returned by
.Fn shm_open ,
is undefined by
POSIX .
It is also undefined whether the shared memory object itself, or its
contents, persist across reboots.
On
.Dx
and most other systems they do not.
Only the
.Dv O_RDONLY ,
.Dv O_RDWR ,
.Dv O_CREAT ,
.Dv O_EXCL ,
and
.Dv O_TRUNC
flags are accepted by the current implementation, in addition to the
internally added
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
Other open flags, including
.Dv O_APPEND ,
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
and
.Dv O_SYNC ,
are rejected with
.Dv EINVAL .
.Sh ERRORS
The
.Fn memfd_create
function can fail with the following errors:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The
.Fa name
argument does not point to a valid string in the calling process.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa name
argument is longer than
.Dv NAME_MAX
characters.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
contains an unsupported flag other than
.Dv MFD_ALLOW_SEALING
or
.Dv MFD_HUGETLB .
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fa flags
contains
.Dv MFD_ALLOW_SEALING
or
.Dv MFD_HUGETLB .
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The per-process limit on open file descriptors has been reached.
.It Bq Er ENFILE
The system-wide limit on open files has been reached.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel could not allocate the backing shared-memory object.
.El
.Pp
The
.Fn shm_open
and
.Fn shm_unlink
functions can fail with any error defined for
.Fn open
and
.Fn unlink ,
respectively.
In addition, the following errors are defined for
.Fn shm_open :
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa path
argument does not begin with
.Ql \&/ .
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The
.Fa path
argument exceeds .Dv NAME_MAX characters.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa flags
argument to
.Fn shm_open
specifies an access mode of
.Dv O_WRONLY .
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa flags
argument contains an unsupported open flag.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr fstat 2 ,
.Xr ftruncate 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr munmap 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr unlink 2 ,
.Xr shmfd 9
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Fn memfd_create
function is expected to be compatible with the Linux system call of the
same name.
.Pp
The
.Fn shm_open
and
.Fn shm_unlink
functions are believed to conform to
.St -p1003.1b-93 .
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn memfd_create
function was added as part of the
.Dx
shmfd project.
.Pp
The
.Fn shm_open
and
.Fn shm_unlink
functions first appeared in
.Fx 4.3 .
